// src/sema/sema_meta.zen
// `@meta`, and the wall in front of it.
//
// The feature is fully designed (`docs/DESIGN.md:453`), lexed
// (`std/lex/lex_scan.at_kind`), parsed in both of its forms
// (`std/parse/parse_expr.meta_expr`), given a node (`ast_node.Meta`) and
// coloured by the editor (`lsp/lsp_colour.zen`) — and implemented by
// nothing at all. `docs/design_meta.md` is the decomposition; this file
// is its first milestone and will be its home.
//
// WHY THE REFUSAL LIVES HERE AND NOT IN THE BACKEND, which is where it
// used to. `gen_c`'s fallthrough said "codegen does not lower this yet:
// `this expression form`" — a sentence about a backend, naming no
// feature, in the one phase that has no business holding a language
// rule. `@scope`, the sibling entry in the same closed namespace, is
// refused in `sema_scope.zen`, and this is that rule's shape.
//
// AND IT WAS TWO DIAGNOSTICS FOR ONE MISTAKE. `expr_kind` had no `Meta`
// arm, so the node fell to `Ty.Unknown`, `Unknown` agrees with
// everything, `@meta(p).name` therefore drew nothing at all, and
// `println` then reported "codegen does not lower this yet: `printing a
// value of this type`" about the poison. Refusing in sema makes it one,
// for free: a sema fault stops the pipeline before `emit`
// (`zen/zen_build.check_tree`).
//
// THE ARGUMENT IS NOT VISITED, deliberately. Descending would report the
// undefined name inside `@meta(bogus)` — a second sentence about a
// program whose first sentence is already "this form does not compile" —
// and it is work the next milestone deletes rather than reuses: once
// `@meta` evaluates, its argument is a comptime term looked up in a
// comptime environment, not an ordinary expression walked by
// `type_of`.
//
// WHY IT IS WORTH ITS OWN FILE at eight lines of code: this refusal is
// the ONLY part of `@meta` the differential oracle will ever be able to
// see. `bootstrap/gen_c.py` refuses it too, and the two are worded to
// share one substring on purpose, so `must-fail/sema/meta_*_form_refused`
// is a rejection both toolchains agree about. Everything after this is
// zen-only by construction — `@meta` is not in the seed subset
// (`docs/PLAN.md:224`) — and `docs/design_meta.md` §6 says what stands
// in for the oracle there.

Expr = std.ast
AllocError = std.mem
TyId = sema.sema_ty
Checker = sema.sema_check
SemaFault, NameFault = sema.sema_diag

// The whole of `@meta` today: one diagnostic at the `@meta` token, then
// poison, so nothing downstream invents a second answer about a form
// that has none.
meta_type* = (c :: Checker, node: Expr) Res<TyId, AllocError> {
    c.report(
        node.span.file,
        node.span,
        SemaFault.MetaNotImplemented(NameFault(name: ""))
    ).try();
    c.types.unknown()
}
